This file tries to explain how the BCP works.

MCND_tm has:
	MCND_Problem;	 //data
	MCND_Solution;	 //best_solution
	BCP_parameter_set<MCND_tm_par>;	 //parameters for tree manager
	BCP_parameter_set<MCND_lp_par>;	 //parameters for lp solver

MCND_lp has:
	MCND_Problem;	 //data
	MCND_Solution;	 //pointer to a solution	
	BCP_parameter_set<MCND_lp_par>;	 //parameters for lp solver

The MAIN function goes into the Problem_TM.cpp:
One have just:    
	<name>_initialize <name>_init;
        bcp_main(argc, argv, &<name>_init);


- bcp_main(argc, argv, &<name>_init) is found in BCP_tm_main.cpp:
	
	USER_initialize (found in BCP_USER.hpp)-  This class is an abstract base class for the initializer class the user
	has to provide. The user will have to return an instance of the
    	initializer class when the BCP_user_init()
    	function is invoked. The member methods of that instance will be invoked
    	to create the various objects (well, pointers to them) that are
    	used/controlled by the user during the course of a run. 
	
